I assessed the relationship between household income, age, race, and geographic region with likelihood of households violating the “half your age plus seven” dating rule. 
The dataset is filtered by heterosexual couples in which the male partner is head of the household and has a female partner.
I estimated two regression models: the first controlling for household income, male partner's age, and geographic region, and the second replacing region with race. 
Both models found that higher household income and older male age were associated with a statistically significant lower probability of dating below the age guideline (p < 0.001). 
In the regional model, some geographic differences were statistically significant, while the racial model identified significant differences across several racial groups relative to White partners. 
